## Brief Overview

---

"Aesthetic cabling," "aesthetic wiring," and "custom wiring setup" already appear in our own package catalog, on the Eco Pack, Wedding Pack, Basic MICE Pack, and MICE Pack respectively, but none of our pages have ever explained what those words mean in practice. This page does: how our crew routes and secures cable at your venue, why that matters for safety and for how your event looks in photos, the real lengths we can run from our own inventory, and where an honestly disclosed extra charge can apply.

Cable management is not a separate service you add to a booking. It is part of the professional setup every package already includes, delivered by the same crew that installs your sound, lighting, or display equipment.

## Key Highlights

---

-   "Aesthetic cabling" is a standard, named inclusion on 4 of our 5 packages: the [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/), [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/), [Basic MICE Pack](/packages/basic-mice/), and [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/).

-   Our inventory includes real cable lengths up to 42 meters for VGA, 30 meters for video, and 25 meters for speaker runs, so most venue layouts fit within what we already stock.

-   A genuinely difficult site or an unusually long cable run can trigger a real installation complement, disclosed here honestly rather than left for the day of your event.

-   We do not trench or bury cable runs, and we do not stock a branded cable ramp or raceway product. Both are worth knowing before you book if either matters to your venue.

-   Cable routing changes with scale: an [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/) party has 2 speakers and 2 light bars to route around, a [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) corporate event has a 60 inch display, dual microphones, and a technician managing the run throughout.

## What Aesthetic Cabling Actually Means

<InlineCTA />

Strip away the marketing phrasing and "aesthetic cabling" describes 2 concrete things our crew does at every setup: cable runs are routed along walls, furniture lines, or the floor edge rather than straight across open walking paths, and any cable crossing a path guests or staff will actually use is secured flat rather than left loose. The photo further down this page, from a corporate lectern setup, shows exactly this: a power run taped flat along the floor, following the wall line to the nearest outlet, gathered and bundled at the equipment itself rather than left as a tangle.

That is the honest scope of it. We are not describing a hidden conduit system or a custom carpentry solution, we are describing careful, consistent routing and securing, done by the same crew that delivers, installs, and tests your equipment, at no separate line item on 4 of our 5 packages.

## Is Cable Management Included in Every Package

The honest answer needs a real table, not a blanket yes, because the wording differs by package and one package's includes list does not name a cabling line at all.

| Package | Price (ex VAT) | Cabling Line in the Includes List | What This Means |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/) | 290 euros | "Aesthetic cabling and professional setup" | Standard inclusion, no technician on site to manage it further |
| [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) | 650 euros | "Professional aesthetic setup and cabling" | Standard inclusion, plus an on-site technician for the whole event |
| [Basic MICE Pack](/packages/basic-mice/) | 295 euros | "...setup, and aesthetic wiring" | Standard inclusion, bundled into transport and setup |
| [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) | 490 euros | "...custom wiring setup..." | Standard inclusion, plus an included technician for up to 6 hours |
| [Product Presentation Pack](/packages/product-presentation/) | 310 euros | Not named as a separate line | See below |

The [Product Presentation Pack](/packages/product-presentation/) is the one honest exception. Its 4-item includes list, a projector and screen, a venue sound system, and a wireless microphone, does not name a cabling or wiring line the way the other 4 packages do. That is not the same as saying cable routing is excluded from it. Every booking we deliver, this package included, is installed by our own crew as part of the setup that price already covers, and the same basic routing habits, cables kept along walls and secured where they cross a path, apply regardless of which package you booked. It is simply not spelled out as its own bullet point in that package's catalog description the way it is on the other 4, and we would rather say that plainly than paper over the gap with a line that is not actually in our own data.

## How Far Can We Run a Cable

Venue layout is usually the real question behind "is cable management included," since a cable that cannot physically reach the outlet or the speaker position is a bigger problem than how neatly it sits once it does. Here is what our own inventory actually stocks, cable type by cable type, longest length first.

| Cable Type | Longest Length We Stock |
| --- | --- |
| VGA | Up to 42 meters |
| Video (Kramer, Christie compatible) | Up to 30 meters |
| Speakon (speaker) | Up to 25 meters |
| Microphone | Up to 20 meters |
| Power extension | Up to 20 meters |
| Sub box | 15 meters |
| Power strip | Up to 15 meters |
| IEC | 1.5 meters |

Most villa terraces, ballrooms, and conference rooms fit comfortably within those lengths, particularly speaker and microphone runs, which have the most headroom. IEC cable is the one short entry in that list by design, it connects directly between an appliance and its own nearby power strip rather than spanning a room, so 1.5 meters is the right length for that job, not a limitation.

## When a Difficult Site Adds an Installation Complement

Here is the part most rental pages leave out entirely: cable management is included in every package's professional setup, but our own price list carries a real, separately valued installation complement for genuinely difficult sites, priced case by case, and our own inventory notes name the exact trigger for it, excessive cabling caused by site difficulty. That is not a hidden fee. It is an honest reflection of the fact that a straightforward hotel ballroom and a villa with 3 separate terrace levels and one distant power point are not the same job, even with the same package booked.

In practice, this rarely surfaces. The vast majority of our bookings fall well inside the lengths in the table above, run through a normal room or garden layout, and the complement never comes up. Tell us your venue layout when you book, distance to power, number of levels, unusual access, and we will tell you honestly whether your site is likely to need it before your event date, not after.

## What We Do Not Do

Being honest about what "aesthetic cabling" includes also means being honest about what it does not. We do not trench or bury cable runs. That is permanent installation work, outside the delivery only model our entire business runs on: we deliver, install, test, and collect, we do not leave anything embedded in a venue after we leave. If your venue genuinely requires buried or under floor cable runs, that is a different service than what we offer.

We also do not stock a named, branded cable ramp or raceway product. Our own inventory does not include one, and we would rather say that directly than let a page imply otherwise. What we do instead is the routing and taping described above, along walls and floor edges, out of primary walking paths, which covers the great majority of venues we work in without needing a dedicated ramp product.

## Cable Management Outdoors

An outdoor venue changes what cable routing has to work around, mainly ground surface and weather exposure, both covered in full in our [audio visual rental for outdoor events](/blog/audio-visual-rental-for-outdoor-events/) guide rather than repeated here. The short version for this page: our crew adapts the same routing and securing habits described above to grass, sand, or uneven terrace ground on site, rather than following one fixed method regardless of surface.

